+    "All of these changes are vendor/btls/btls-sys.patch. BoringSSL takes patches/browser-fingerprint.patch after upstream's own patches: SSL_CTX_set_grease_sigalgs_enabled and SSL_CTX_set1_advertised_sigalgs list a GREASE value and algorithms only declared ahead of the verification preferences in the ClientHello's signature_algorithms, as Chrome 154 does",
+    "The build script names only the library directories the build made, so the linker warns about none missing (Zig's, for every Linux target)",
+    "Every Windows build, from any build machine, compiles BoringSSL without assembly (OPENSSL_NO_ASM), as upstream already did on a Windows build machine, and takes the C runtime from crt-static even under a CMake toolchain file such as cargo-xwin's: the x86-64 assembly would need NASM, and the toolchain file's early return dropped the runtime choice",
+    "An MSVC build generates for Ninja, as cargo-xwin's does, so BoringSSL compiles with the build's compiler and the MSVC toolset its environment selected: cmake's default Visual Studio generator compiled with Visual Studio's newest toolset (14.51) instead of the pinned 14.44, whose static C++ library then lacked the ARM64 vectorized algorithms the newer STL headers called (__std_find_trivial_1 and others), so aarch64-pc-windows-msvc failed to link on a Windows runner; disabling _USE_STD_VECTOR_ALGORITHMS would hide that symptom but still link one toolset's objects against an older one's libraries",
+    "suspicious_runtime_symbol_definitions is allowed: the bindings declare the C library's malloc and realloc with C's types, which the pinned nightly's lint flags, and nothing calls them",
+]
